<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Pforzheim</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Pforzheim is a city at the northern edge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. It was a residence of the margraves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, and from 1767 it developed a major jewellery and watchmaking industry, earning the name 'Gold City'. Most of the city was destroyed in an air raid on 23 February 1945.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/pforzheim.jpg" alt="Pforzheim" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Paralacre · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panorama_Nagold,_Stadtkirche_und_Enz_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Pforzheim lies at the northern rim of the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> and the edge of the hilly Kraichgau, in an open valley where the rivers Enz, Nagold and Würm meet. Because of this it is called the three-valleys town and the Gateway to the Black Forest. It stands about halfway between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stuttgart">Stuttgart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karlsruhe">Karlsruhe</a>, on the border of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> and Württemberg, on the Baden side. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> settled the site earlier than the present centres of Stuttgart and Karlsruhe, and built a ford just past the confluence of the three rivers for their military highway.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Its position later made Pforzheim a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timber</a>-rafting trade, which carried logs from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-forest">Black Forest</a> down the Enz, Nagold and Würm, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neckar">Neckar</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, to markets that included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, where the timber was used in shipbuilding and for the foundations of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amsterdam">Amsterdam</a>. Guilds of tailors, bakers, weavers, wine-growers, skippers and timber raftsmen were founded between 1410 and 1512. In 1501 Margrave Christoph I of Baden-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> issued an ordinance on the rafting profession. Margrave Karl II installed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation</a> in the Margraviate of Baden in 1556, when Pforzheim was its capital, and the town stayed Protestant for centuries.</p><p>In 1618 Pforzheim had an estimated 2,500 to 3,000 inhabitants and was the largest town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a>. In 1645, near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>, Bavarian troops burned the old town. In 1718 an institution for orphans, the sick and the poor opened in the former Dominican convent by the Enz; some fifty years later it became the starting point of the town's jewellery and watchmaking industries. In 1836 Ferdinand Öchsle invented a device for measuring the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> content of grape juice, still used in winemaking. Watchmaking revived from 1900 and thrived in the 1920s.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Pforzheim is a city of more than 135,000 inhabitants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, known as the Goldstadt for its jewellery and watchmaking. On the evening of 23 February 1945 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-air-force">Royal Air Force</a> carried out the largest of its air raids on the city, one of the most devastating area bombardments of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>. About 17,600 people, nearly a third of the population, were killed, and about 83 per cent of the buildings were destroyed. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a> believed that precision instruments were made there and that the town was a transport centre.</p><p>After French occupation it was administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> from 1945 to 1948, and was rebuilt over the following twenty years. The rubble was heaped on the Wallberg hill, where a monument stands.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Pforzheim” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ernest received the northern lands around <b>Pforzheim</b> and the southern Upper Margraviate around Hochberg and Rötteln; his line moved its residence to Durlach in 1565 and became known as Baden-Durlach.</blockquote><a href="/state/margraviate-of-baden-durlach">Margraviate of Baden-Durlach</a></li></ul></section>
